HekateBookPrologue: {
	Page1:
		'''
		Idri has proven to have quite some potential.
		He is a fierce fighter, and has cleared every dungeon I've
		sent him to. And you'd think, for someone having been raised
		as a court jester for Mother Darkness of all people, that he'd
		be jaded and scornful of the world (I've met many such people).
		'''
	Page2:
		'''
		But no, Idri has a young, optimistic outlook on the future.
		He desires to right the world's wrongs as best he can, and he
		is dedicated to uprooting tyranny wherever it rises. Where
		I'd call any other young man with that outlook naïve,
		I call Idri the future of freedom and peace.
		'''
	Page3:
		'''
		Shopping List:
		- Salves
		- A stack of blessed paper <- GET THE GOOD KIND
		- Bread
		- Parsnips
		- Leeks
		- Goat Milk
		'''
}

HekateBook1: {
	Page1:
		'''
		Idri's been concerning me as of late. I've had to treat hives
		on his skin day-in and day-out. He hasnt been able to meet with
		the group for weeks, citing a newfound lethargy, and it's been
		detrimental to the planning some of our more ambitious plans,
		since he tended to be our voice of reason.
		'''
	Page2:
		'''
		I write this as I lay beside him in bed. He's fallen asleep, but
		the restless shuddering of his torso tells me he isn't sleeping
		easy. I've gotten him soups from all varieties of market, but
		nothing cures his mysterious aliment, and no text could ever 
		know of diseases affecting demideities.
		'''
	Page3:
		'''
		I may consult mother darkness on the matter, but I'm hesitant. 
		Justifiedly so. She would be furious if she knew it had been me
		that had taken her son from her, and I get the feeling her
		mercy to old friends only extends so far. Goodness, I don't
		know how many other options I have.
		'''
}

HekateBook2: {
	Page1:
		'''
		Idri's aliment seems to have become a particular issue.
		He's left his quarters less frequently, and his hives have
		spread to his arms. He has begun to refuse my help in treating
		the hives, but has not said why. Unfortunately, I must do
		what I can to help him, and have had to restrain him on multiple
		occasions to apply the ointment.
		'''
	Page2: His condition scares me. It really does. I fear losing him.
}

HekateBook3: {
	Page1:
		'''
		I wept for the first time in centuries this morning. And not
		the usual sob or choke-up. I had gotten a new understaning of
		mortality as I slump against the wall, wailing a discordant
		chime and clenching my eyes shut. It was as if I didnt want
		to turn to face the reality that already had turned to face me.
		'''
	Page2:
		'''
		As I had predicted, yet so foolishly ignored, Idri's breathing
		has become more strained, and his illness has worsened depsite
		my regenerative talismans. Foolish of me. Demigod or not, I
		should have stayed by his side all through that night instead
		of leaving him in his cold, lonely room.
		'''
	Page3:
		'''
		I haven't felt things so rawly in so long a time. It perplexes
		me as much as it moves me. Deities are beyond emotion; that's
		a commonly known fact. So how is it that I grieve? How is it
		that I weep so loud and so long that my chest throbs sorely and
		my eyes are burned by my caustic fluids? Perhaps we need to
		rethink our defintions of Deitism.
		'''
}

HekateBook4: {
	Page1:
		'''
		HE'S GONE.
		THE ROSE I STORED IDRI'S SOUL IN. IT'S GONE.
		GONE GONE GONE GONE GONE.
		I DON'T KNOW WHO STOLE IT OR WHEN, BUT I CAN'T FIND IT.
		IM SUCH A HORRIBLE, ROTTEN GOD.
		SMITE ME. SMITE ME, NOW, GREAT STARS OF THE SKY.
		'''
	Page2:
		'''
		After taking a moment to collect myself after my emotional
		scribbling on  the last page, I know what I will do now.

		I will set off to find Idri's Rose. 
		Even if it takes me millenia, I will find it, and I will
		enjoy the sunset with Idri again.
		'''
	Page3:
		'''
		I will leave my garden as it remains now.
		The talismans I have applied should work to keep the
		place safe from any encroaching forces like the corruption.
		'''
}
